    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3410.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 752,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we normally drink and what fills our oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost. \n*   **Gas (Vapor or Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam rising from a boiling pot.\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several fascinating properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (in our blood, through plant roots, etc.).\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable in living organisms.\n*   **Anomalous Expansion:** Most substances shrink when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water instead of sinking to the bottom of the ocean—a phenomenon that prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in the oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh water. Furthermore, over two-thirds of that fresh water is locked away in frozen glaciers and polar ice caps, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into the oceans. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water:\n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals.\n*   **Human Society:** Water is essential for agriculture (growing food), generating electricity (hydropower), manufacturing goods, and sanitation.\n\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with complex, miraculous properties that make existence possible."
